/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In a single-plate clutch a steel plate lined on both faces is clamped between the engine flywheel and a pressure plate by springs giving the axial force W.
   - A cone clutch uses a shallow cone of semi-angle α, so the normal force is W/sin α and the same spring gives more torque.
   - The friction torque depends on how the pressure p is spread over the ring between the inner radius ri and the outer radius ro.
   - uniform wear (any clutch in service), with μ the coefficient of friction, W in N and radii in m, giving T in N·m.
   - T = (2/3)μWn(ro³ − ri³)/(ro² − ri²) uniform pressure (a new, accurately flat lining).
   - Uniform pressure puts more of the force at large radius, so it gives slightly more torque; a designer uses uniform wear, the lower and safer value.
   - A brake holds one friction member still: the drum or disc turns with the wheel and the block or pad is fixed to the frame.
   - is the heat in joules for steady slip at relative speed ωrel (rad/s) for t seconds.
   - The heat is first stored in the drum or plate, then carried away by air.
*/
