/* book Worked example: Q: Design the I-section of a steel connecting rod for an engine with bore 100 mm, peak gas pressure 4 MPa and rod length 240 mm between centres. Take a factor of safety of 6, σc = 330 MPa and a = 1/7500. Also find the small-end pin and big-end crankpin sizes for pb = 12.5 MPa with l/d = 2 and pb = 10 MPa with l/d = 1.
   1. Gas force: Fp = (π/4) × 100² × 4 = 31 416 N.
   2. Buckling load needed: Pcr = 6 × 31 416 = 188 496 N.
   3. Rankine with A = 11t², kxx² = 3.17t²: 188 496 = 330 × 11t²/(1 + (1/7500) × 240²/(3.17t²)).
   4. Simplify: 3630t² = 188 496 + 456 070/t², so 3630t⁴ − 188 496t² − 456 060 = 0. Solving the quadratic in t² gives t² = 54.24 mm², t = 7.37 mm.
   5. Adopt t = 8 mm: B = 32 mm, H = 40 mm, A = 704 mm², kxx² = 203.2 mm². Then Pcr = 330 × 704/(1 + 240²/(7500 × 203.2)) = 223 860 N, a factor of safety of 7.1.
   6. Small end: d² = Fp/(2pb) = 31 416/(2 × 12.5) = 1257 mm², d = 35.4 mm; adopt 36 mm × 72 mm.
   7. Big end: d² = Fp/pb = 31 416/10 = 3142 mm², d = 56.0 mm; adopt 56 mm × 56 mm.
   Answer: I-section t = 8 mm, B = 32 mm, H = 40 mm (about 44 to 50 mm at the big end, 30 to 36 mm at the small end); piston pin 36 × 72 mm; crankpin 56 × 56 mm. */
